+# Local wireless over the network (LAN)
+
+BananaDS plays a DS game's local wireless (tested with Mario Kart DS's local groups and races; other games are untested) between
+handhelds, or a handheld and desktop melonDS 1.0/1.1, on the same Wi-Fi network, through melonDS's LAN (ENet, UDP
+7064 for the game and 7063 for the hosts' broadcasts, once a second). It needs `-DBANANADS_LAN=ON` and libenet
+(BananaLinux builds it so). With no saved `wifi.local`, local wireless starts in Auto; a saved value is kept. The
+settings are in [SETTINGS.md](SETTINGS.md) (`wifi.local`, `wifi.local_players`, `firmware.nickname`).
+This build always uses the network the console is already on. It has no Connection row and it does not call
+`wifi-link`. A saved `wifi.local_link` is ignored. The latency warning is hidden when an address is in
+`10.42.0.0/16` (another app's direct link). Hosts are found by a broadcast to `255.255.255.255` (melonDS
+`LAN::ProcessDiscovery`), so any subnet works, including `10.42.0.0/16`.
+
+Desktop melonDS's own LAN dialog warns, and so does the menu's page: LAN requires low network latency to work; do
+not expect it to work through a VPN or any sort of tunnel.
+
+## The in-game menu's Local multiplayer page (Quick settings > Local multiplayer)
+
+- **No Connection row.** Mode is the first row. Play is over the Wi-Fi network the console is on: Host on one,
+  Join or Auto on the others.
+- **Mode: Off / Auto / Auto Join / Host / Join**, changed while the game runs, without a restart, and kept in
+  `bananads.conf` (`wifi.local = off | auto | host | join`; `join` looks for hosts at the start, `join:<address>`
+  joins that host directly). Auto Join (`wifi.local = autojoin`, not kept by the page, like Auto) is Auto that never
+  hosts: it looks for a host and joins it, and keeps looking until it finds one. `BANANADS_WIFI_LOCAL` / `BANANADS_WIFI_LOCAL_PLAYERS` in the environment still win at each start, and
+  the page says so in red.
+- **Players** (2-16, 4 by default, `wifi.local_players`): only with Host on the Mode row; greyed, "Turn Host on to
+  change it", until Host is on, and while others are in its session. Auto hosts with the same count.
+- **Hosts found** (Join only): the hosts heard on the network, with their name, players and address; A joins one
+  ("Connecting..." for up to 5 s; the page says why a join failed, and goes on looking). The list is always
+  "Hosts on this network". There is no Nearby consoles list and no direct-link progress.
+- **Disconnect** (only on the Mode row of the mode that is on, Host or Join): a host's session ends; a player goes
+  back to looking for hosts. Auto has none: Off leaves at once, and Auto again starts afresh.
+- A status box: what the mode is doing, the session's players with their ping, desktop melonDS's warning (with
+  Auto, Host or Join on the Mode row, Router only) and the name the others see (the DS name, Settings > Nintendo
+  DS). With Direct: the link's progress while it starts (below), "In <host>'s session" and "Connecting to <host>..."
+  by the host's DS name instead of its address, and instead of the latency warning that the console has no internet
+  while the link is on.
+- Another mode picked while others are in the session (Host with a player, Join or Auto in a host's session,
+  Auto hosting players) asks first ("Disconnect from Host?"; No keeps the mode). During a local wireless game only
+  Off (Auto) or Disconnect works, after a question.
+- Without a network (an interface up and running with an IPv4 address, looked for every second) nothing starts:
+  Quick settings' row says "No Wi-Fi" and doesn't open, the mode is kept, and it starts again when the network is
+  back. A session ends when the network goes. Nintendo WFC (slirp) isn't affected. Without a network the page
+  does not open.
+
+## Direct link (not in this build)
+
+The rest of this section describes Connection: Direct, which this branch does not build in. That behavior is on
+`local-multiplayer-0.5.0.1`. This build never runs `wifi-link`.
+
+## Direct link (Connection: Direct)
+
+Tried by hand on an RG DS and an RG DS Plus (BananaOS 0.5.0.1, 2026-10-29): several games played over the link,
+steadier than over the home router there. Auto with Direct, a sleep with the link up and more than two consoles
+are still untested. With Direct, Host, Join and Auto first run `link.script`
+(`/data/store/apps/bananads-mp/wifi-link`, the copy the multiplayer app installs;
+`BANANADS_LINK_SCRIPT` in the environment overrides it) as
+`/bin/sh <script> up host|join --owner <BananaDS's pid> [--channel N]` on a thread of its own, wait for it (up to
+60 s; the status box shows its progress, below) and then use the link for the hosts'
+broadcasts and ENet as on a router. The script:
+
+- takes the Wi-Fi off the home network (BananaOS's `wifi down`, holding its `/run/banana/wifi.lock` while the link
+  is up) and joins or starts an IBSS ("ad hoc") cell: SSID `bananalink`, BSSID 02:42:4E:41:00:01, 2.4 GHz channel 1
+  (`wifi.local_link_channel` 1-11, or `CHANNEL=` in `/data/banana/wifi-link.conf`). Every console must use the same
+  channel.
+- No DHCP: the host takes 10.42.0.1/16 (another address if it is taken), a player 10.42.A.B made from its wlan0
+  MAC (A, B 1-254), each checked first with an ARP probe and moved on a clash, so 4-16 consoles don't collide. A
+  default route on wlan0 (no gateway) lets the broadcasts out.
+- The cell is looked for before it is joined (its own scans until a fresh beacon of the BSSID: a player up to 15 s,
+  a host 3 s), and a player checks every 10 s that the host (10.42.0.1) answers an ARP request; while it doesn't,
+  it leaves the cell, looks for it again (35 s at most) and joins again. With both consoles busy with the game,
+  mac80211's own two short scans can miss the host's cell; it then starts one of its own with the same BSSID, which
+  it never merges and which may not hear the host (the first test on the handhelds: "looking for hosts" for good).
+- A host that receives nothing starts its cell again every 20 s. Alone for 30 s, mac80211 scans for a cell to
+  merge with, and on these handhelds (rtw88_8821cs) the host's cell is then no longer heard: a player that comes
+  later starts its own and never gets the host's discovery broadcasts, though ARP and ping still pass (the second
+  test on the handhelds, 2026-10-29). A player's own cell, if it started one, is adopted at the next start.
+- Open cell: BananaOS's wpa_supplicant is built without IBSS RSN, so the cell is not encrypted and anyone in
+  reach can join it while it is up. The console has no internet meanwhile (the page says so).
+- Puts the home Wi-Fi back (only if it was up before) on Off, a host's Disconnect, Router, quitting the game,
+  BananaDS crashing (a supervisor watches its pid), a sleep (BananaOS's sleep.d `05-wifi-link`, and the supervisor
+  sees BananaDS stopped), its wpa_supplicant dying, or the supervisor itself being killed (a watchdog). A player's
+  Disconnect keeps the link and looks for hosts again. Its log: `/data/banana/logs/wifi-link.log`.
+
+While the link starts, the script writes its phase in its state file (`phase=` in `/run/banana/wifi-link/state`,
+also in `wifi-link status`; reporting only, its steps and timings are unchanged), and the page shows it with the
+seconds since the link began (the Quick settings row: "Linking, N s"): `leaving` ("Leaving your Wi-Fi network...
+N s"), `search` ("Searching for the host..." for a player, "Starting the link..." for a host), `joining` ("Host
+found, joining its link..."), `starting` (a player that heard no host and starts the cell itself: "No host heard
+yet: starting the link..."); a script that writes no phase gives "Starting the direct link... N s". This display is
+checked in the menu harness (lan_direct_test) only, not yet watched on the handhelds.
+
+A link that fails (no cell after 20 s, the script failing or not answering) turns the mode Off with the reason in red; a
+link that goes away while on (two missed checks a second apart) does the same. A kept `wifi.local` other than
+`off` with Direct doesn't start the link at a game's start: the console never loses its network without a choice
+on the page.
+
+## Auto
+
+Every console in the room on Auto (or one on Host and the rest on Auto) is enough. Nothing happens until the game
+turns its wifi on (its multiplayer menu); then it listens 2 s plus up to 1 s at random and joins a
+host of the same game (the discovery beacon's game tag, melonDS patch 0002; one hosting by hand first, else the
+lowest address), else a desktop melonDS host if there is no tagged one, else hosts. Of two that start together,
+the one with the higher address joins the other. Nothing changes during a local wireless game; a player whose host
+leaves looks again; a game that goes online (Nintendo WFC) gets no session. A host alone whose game turns its wifi
+off for 3 s of game time (it left its multiplayer mode; Mario Kart DS turns it off and on for under 2 s between its
+own screens) ends its session and waits; with others in it, after 30 s off. A UDP port already taken stops Auto
+("Auto: stopped") until the game's wifi goes off and on, or Off then Auto.
+
+## The game around it
+
+- While the menu has the game paused, the emulator thread services the LAN session every 60 ms, so ENet doesn't
+  time the others out. During a local wireless game the menu doesn't pause it at all: the game gets neutral input
+  and the sound plays at half volume, Save/Load state, Restart and Cheats are off, and a session that ends while
+  the menu is open pauses the game then (`kms.menu_live = off`: the old behaviour).
+- Run-ahead (`runahead`, `kms.lowlat=runahead1`/`runahead2`) doesn't run while a LAN session or discovery is on
+  (`Emu::runahead_ok`), and a frame run ahead sends and takes in nothing on the LAN.
+- `wfcsettings.bin` (Nintendo WFC's settings) is saved, atomically, in desktop melonDS's layout, so the WFC ID
+  isn't registered again after every restart.
+- Each console's DS MAC is BananaDS's own (00:09:BF plus the FNV-1a of `/etc/machine-id`), in the generated firmware
+  and in the user's firmware (`boot=firmware`) alike, so two handhelds don't clash and every BananaDS on a console is
+  the same DS to a game's WFC ID.
+
+## Pixelado's melonDS patches (pixelado-cores: ds/patches/melonds)
+
+Numbered on their own from 0001 and applied after all of BananaDS's (bananads/patches/melonds), in name order.
+
+- **0001** (Wifi): the end-of-window power-down rule. A Mario Kart DS parent stays awake after its beacons (1.1 put
+  it to sleep, so its children's requests rarely landed), a child follows 1.1's rule (906e9eb's kept it awake when
+  it had to sleep, so it could never join), WFC keeps 906e9eb's rule, dswifi's mode 2 without auto wake-up stays on
+  (#2680/#2681).
+- **0002** (LAN): the host waits only for the players in its DS group (one in its menu or between races no longer
+  drops everyone to ~38 fps for ~16 s); players who join late learn that our wifi is on; accessors for the frontend;
+  one session after another in the same run; the game tag and Status in the discovery beacon (same size and
+  layout: stock melonDS shows the name as before); the others hear at once that we leave.
+- **0002** (LAN): a child repeats its auth/association request every frame until the parent answers (1 s at most).
+  Desktop melonDS 1.0/1.1 hosting a group sleeps between its beacons and drops a frame that came while it slept,
+  so a single request reached it only by chance; a parent that stays awake answers before the first copy.
+- **0003** (LAN, for testing): `BANANADS_LAN_TIMEOUT_MS` (1-100, in /data/banana/bananads.env) replaces the 25 ms
+  the host waits for its players' replies to each MP command, and a child for the host (unset: 25 ms, as before).
+  The value in use is logged at startup ("LAN: MP receive timeout"); with the variable set, the per-second
+  "[diag] LAN last" line comes at the Info level on both sides, with the waits (count, total, longest) and the
+  host's reply waits that timed out.
+- **0004** (LAN, for testing, diag-respuesta): with `BANANADS_LAN_TIMEOUT_MS` set, a second per-second line,
+  "[diag] LAN resp" (times in ms, mean/p95[/max]): `frames` in the window, `run` (RunFrame's time, its LAN waits
+  included), `emu` (the same minus those waits), `sleep` (the pacer's, in the window, from the frontend); on the host
+  `rtt` (a MP command sent to all its replies in) and `pre` (the command sent to the start of that wait); on a child
+  `reply` (the host's command handed to its emulation to its reply sent).
+- **0005** (LAN, for testing, diag-respuesta-temprana; no behavior change): with `BANANADS_LAN_TIMEOUT_MS` set, a
+  child logs "[diag] LAN early" once a second: its reply slot (`W_TXSlotReply1`, the frame, its length, valid and
+  short enough for the CMD's reply time) snapshotted when the host's CMD arrives (`arr`) and when its reception starts
+  (`rx`), against what `SendMPReply` sends at the end of that reception: `ok` (the same frame, less the TX header and
+  the sequence control), `blank` (no reply either time), `pay` (another payload; `pay offs`, the first differing
+  802.11 offsets of up to 4 `arr` samples), `len`, `notready` (no valid slot then, one at the reply), `gone` (the
+  reverse), `nosnap`; `validatarr`; `slot1 writes` (of `W_TXSlotReply1`, its latch and reset included) and `buf
+  writes` (into the snapshotted frame) between the CMD's arrival and the reply, and in how many replies.
+- **0006** (LAN, respuesta-temprana; on unless `BANANADS_LAN_EARLY_REPLY=0`): a child sends its reply to the host's
+  CMD from `CheckRX` as the CMD arrives, instead of at the end of the CMD's emulated reception, built as that end
+  would: the reply slot's frame (`SendMPReply`'s checks, `TXSendFrame`'s sequence number, retry bit, WEP FCS and
+  channel), else the default empty reply, or the blank one if the CMD isn't for us; stamped with the time the
+  reception ends (`RXTimestamp` plus the CMD's air time), which the host's `RecvReplies` checks against its own
+  (the CMD's end, -32 us). The end of the reception keeps every emulated side effect but sends nothing: what it would
+  have sent is compared with what went out. "[diag] LAN early-reply", a second: what went out (`frame`, `default`,
+  `blank`), then `ok` and the mismatches (`kind`, `len`, `hdr` (bytes 0-11), `seq`, `pay`, the first `offs`),
+  `orphan` (a reception that never ended) and the late - early time stamps in us. A mismatch is never resent.
+- **0006** (LAN, respuesta-temprana): only a frame already in the reply slot, for our AID, goes out early; a default
+  or blank reply is left to the end of the reception, which sends it as without the flag. What the reply is depends
+  on the game's state at that end: Mario Kart DS's lobby ("Espera, por favor" before "Guardando") turns `W_AIDLow` 0
+  and 1 again around every CMD, and with 0007 alone 2 of 3 early blanks were frames at the end (two handhelds over
+  the direct link, 2026-10-29: the host got blanks, the session failed before "Guardando"). The diag line adds
+  `left to the reception's end default= blank=` and `early frames late as default= blank=`.
+- **0007** (LAN, DS Download Play host, an experiment, off): with `BANANADS_LAN_DLPLAY_PIPELINE=1`, while we send
+  DS Download Play beacons (type 0Bh in Nintendo's DDh element), `RecvReplies` doesn't wait for the players'
+  replies: each MP command gets the replies already in, up to 100 ms old, and a player that doesn't reply isn't
+  counted out. The download sends ~10 MP commands a frame, and the round trip over Wi-Fi for each (3-5 ms between
+  two handhelds through a router) takes the host to 20-30 fps. Without the wait the host keeps its speed, but the
+  download never completes (two handhelds, the child stays on "Downloading data..."): a reply that answered an
+  earlier command is taken, and acknowledged in the host's ACK, as the answer to the current one, so the child
+  believes its newer reply arrived and the two sides of the transfer no longer agree. A DS MP command needs its
+  own replies before the host's ACK, so the wait stays unless the variable is set. The usual wait comes back 2 s
+  after the last such beacon ("LAN: DS Download Play host: ..." in the log at both changes).
+- **0001** (Wifi, always on): a transceiver the game turns off is no longer a MP client (`IsMP`, `IsMPClient` and
+  the sync time cleared, "[CLIENT] wifi off: no longer a MP client" in the log). A DS Download Play child that
+  reboots into the downloaded program turns its wifi off and on, and with Mario Party DS neither side sends a
+  deauth first: the client flag stayed set, so every Wi-Fi tick past the sync time asked for a host frame and
+  waited the full MP receive timeout (25 ms) for a host that no longer sent it any. The child's frames took
+  seconds (black screen, the host's "communication error"), it never read the host's new beacons to join the
+  game, and F + START waited for the frame in progress (~10 s). Mario Kart DS ends the download with a deauth,
+  which already cleared the flag. A powered-off transceiver keeps no association on the real hardware either.
+- **0003** (LAN, on unless `BANANADS_LAN_ENET_THROTTLE=1`): ENet's packet throttle is kept at its top on every
+  peer (deceleration 0, "LAN: ENet packet throttle off" in the log). ENet drops unreliable and unsequenced packets
+  (all the MP commands, replies and acks) in proportion to how far the throttle is under its limit, and it steps
+  the throttle down each time a reliable round trip (its pings, every 500 ms) comes back slower than the last plus
+  twice its variance, which Wi-Fi through a router does every few seconds (3-33 ms between two handhelds). MP
+  packets are never resent: each one dropped costs the side waiting for it the full MP receive timeout (25 ms). A
+  game that sends several MP commands a frame (Mario Party DS: ~180 a second, against ~55 for Mario Kart DS's
+  race) pays it most. With `BANANADS_LAN_TIMEOUT_MS` set, "[diag] LAN enet:" adds each peer's ENet round trip and
+  throttle (32 of 32: nothing dropped) to the per-second lines.
+- **0001** (Wifi, always on): a MP client whose host went quiet keeps running. When the host's game quits and its
+  session stays up (a Banana Party room keeps it, and its room re-announces itself), nothing tells the client the
+  host is gone: every Wi-Fi tick past the sync time waited the full MP receive timeout for a host frame, and the
+  sync time only moves with one, so emulated time stood still (one frame took ~52 s: 2069 waits of 25 ms), the
+  picture froze and F + START waited for the frame. Now after 4 timeouts in a row the next wait is a frame (16 ms)
+  of emulated time away, so the game runs on and meets the lost link as a DS does (its own error screen), and after
+  3 s with nothing from the host it is no longer a MP client ("[CLIENT] the host has sent nothing for ... ms" in the
+  log). A host frame resets the count; a hiccup of a timeout or two waits as before.
+- Their other "[diag]" traces log at the Debug level only (`log.level = 0`).
